<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong De</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">336–405</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> De was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> prince, younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> of Later Yan, and founder of Southern Yan. When Northern Wei took the Later Yan capital Zhongshan in 397, he led followers south, proclaimed himself King of Yan in 398 and later emperor, ruling from Guanggu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murong-de.jpg" alt="Murong De" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see below · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_49802792_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E7%BA%8C%E7%B7%A8_v.1_(%E6%85%95%E5%AE%B9%E5%BE%B7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murong De was born in 336 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang">Murong Huang</a> and his concubine Consort Gongsun; in 337 his father claimed the title Prince of Yan, an act generally taken as the founding of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> state. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a>, after claiming the imperial title in 354, made him Duke of Liang, and he later became Prince of Fanyang. In 369 he served under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> in the defeat of the Jin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> near Yecheng, but when Murong Chui fled to Former Qin that year, Murong De lost his post as mayor of the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Former Qin conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> in 370 he served its emperor Fu Jiān as governor of Zhangye Commandery. After Fu Jiān's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Battle of Fei River</a> he urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> to kill him and restore Yan. When Murong Chui did rebel in 384 and founded Later Yan, the governor at Zhangye executed Murong De's brother Murong Na and all of Murong De's sons. Under Murong Chui he was a chief official and general, campaigning near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and against the Xianbei Helan tribe in 389. He served at the disastrous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-canhe-slope">Battle of Canhe Slope</a> in 395, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-bao">Murong Bao</a> he was viceroy at Yecheng, which he held against several attacks by Northern Wei.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On the advice of his nephew Murong Lin he abandoned Yecheng in spring 398 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-county">Huatai</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, where he declined the imperial title but founded Southern Yan; he soon executed Murong Lin for plotting rebellion. After losing Huatai to Northern Wei in 399 he advanced east and that autumn took Guanggu, capital of the Jin province of Qing, from its governor Pilü Hun. He claimed the imperial title in 400, changing his name to Murong Beide to ease the naming taboo, and thereafter carried out few campaigns. He refused a proposal to attack Jin in 403 and survived a plot against him in 404.</p><p>In 405 Murong Na's posthumously born son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chao">Murong Chao</a> reached Southern Yan and proved who he was with the golden knife that Murong De had given his mother. Having no surviving sons, Murong De made him crown prince shortly before his death. His burial was secret: more than 10 caskets were carried out of the gates of Guanggu, only one of them holding his body.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murong De” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A branch under <b>Murong De</b> founded Southern Yan.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-yan">Later Yan</a></li><li><blockquote>When Northern Wei took the Later Yan capital Zhongshan in 397, <b>Murong De</b>, a younger brother of Murong Chui, led followers south to Huatai, proclaiming himself King of Yan in 398.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-yan">Southern Yan</a></li></ul></section>
